        # About

        My name's Vladimir and on the Internet, I go by "allejo."

        You may recognize me as a current/former coworker that you liked, a mentor from a tech community, a shitposter on LinkedIn, or as a contributor to open source projects. I'm a senior software engineer currently in higher ed doing frontend development at [UC Merced](https://www.ucmerced.edu), previously I was at [Stack Overflow](https://stackoverflow.com/).

        I've been writing code since middle school and accidentally ended up with two decades of experience in software engineering. As many engineers in tech can probably relate, I would like to retire young to a remote village, dedicate myself to [apiculture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beekeeping), have my own garden, my jailbroken Kindle, and a gaming PC with no network card so I can finally beat all my favorite childhood video games. If I want Wi-Fi, I'll go to the city and find myself a public library. I loathe technology and what it has become; the forced romanticization of everything in social media, the loss of critical thinking because of AI, the mass spread of misinformation, and the crime against humanity that is reality TV.

        What I do like about technology is building developer tools like documentation builders, code generators, syntax highlighters, pipeline automation, and similar tooling. I also enjoy building and designing 3D worlds as a hobby, so I've enjoyed contributing to BZFlag, its community, and ecosystem over the past decade. I strongly believe that technology can be used for good, so I do pro bono or discounted work for nonprofits, open source projects, and small businesses. If you have a project that you think I could help with, reach out to me via email or LinkedIn.

        [I speak at conferences]({{ url(pages['Resume']) }}#conference-talks) and workplaces about accessibility, mentoring, frontend, and technical leadership. Teaching others is a passion of mine, and I enjoy helping others grow in their careers. I also enjoy writing about my experiences in software engineering, so I write articles on [my blog]({{ url(pages['Blog']) }}).

        ## Mentoring

        Since 2019, I have mentored 30+ junior and 10+ mid-level developers. With AI on the rise, I believe now more than ever, we should be focusing on training junior and mid-level developers. Once the current generation of seniors retire or get promoted, we won't have anyone in the industry who's been learning how to fill those roles. I make it a goal to always mentor junior developers in every position I go.

        - While at the City of Santa Monica, I helped run the Youth Tech Program where I taught high school students how to build websites.
        - While at California State University, Northridge, I started my own student development team that had over 10 student hires in my three-year tenure.
        - While at Stack Overflow, I volunteered as a mentor at Techtonica—a bootcamp to help women and LGBTQ+ transition into tech careers.
        - While at UC Merced, I started the departments first student development team and in under 2 years, have hired 8+ students; with 4 being undocumented students I was able to work with via grants.

        My next goal is to start lecturing at the college level for computer science, software engineering, web design, and HCI/accessibility classes.

